{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:693c559e842aadf93ddcaba5f140c97aac1419c23fda3c1038c7a690c13b1213", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Section 1\nThe Guardian has issued several corrections to their recent articles. An article about a new pet theft law stated that it applied to the UK, but it actually applies to England and Northern Ireland. Another article referred to Eileen Burbidge as the founder of Fertifa, when in fact she is the executive director, and the founders are Tony Chen and Nick Kuan. The Guardian also corrected an interview with Juliet Stevenson, where they misspelled the director's name as \"Lowther\" instead of \"Lawther\", and incorrectly stated that the family in the film Bend It Like Beckham are Muslim, when they are actually Sikh.\n\n*** \nSection 2\nThe corrections continue with an article about Venetia Stanley's relationship with Herbert Asquith, where they stated that her father converted to Islam, when in fact it was her uncle who did so. The Guardian has also amended several other articles, including ones about the Telegram app founder, royal servants, and the Pope's thoughts on England's cricket team. Some of the key corrections include:\n* The new pet theft law applies to England and Northern Ireland, not the entire UK.\n* Eileen Burbidge is the executive director of Fertifa, not the founder, and the family in the film Bend It Like Beckham are Sikh, not Muslim. \nSection 3\nThe Guardian's corrections are an important part of their commitment to accuracy and transparency. By issuing these corrections, they are able to provide their readers with the most accurate information possible. The corrections cover a wide range of topics, from politics and crime to entertainment and sports. \nSection 4\nThe Guardian's readers can contact the Readers' Editor with any questions or concerns about the corrections. P.P.S", "logprobs": [{"token": "Section", "logprob": -0.000297144608339294